-- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n |  |  | This comment will address these forms in two\nparts the non-governmental form FL-334 and\nthe governmental forms FL-680, and FL-686.\nA. FL-334 Declaration Re Address\nVerification – Postjudgment Request Modify\nA Child Custody, Visitation, Or Child\nSupport Order\nThis form is clearly based upon the form MC-\n052 as suggested by the Elkins Commission.\nHowever, this form contains some significant\ndefects. It is important to understand the\ndifference between purposes for each of these\ntwo forms. Form MC-052 is a form designed to\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTitle: (https://courts.ca.gov/sites/default/files/courts/default/2024-12/2011srl6aitc.pdf)\nciteturn1693search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.4 years ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; Modeled after the existing form *Declaration in Support of Attorney’s Motion to Be Relieved as Counsel—Civil* (form MC-052), form FL-334 would require that the declarant verify the current address of the party being served within the 30 days immediately preceding the signing of the declaration and specify how he or she verified that address. ... Additional space would be provided for the declarant to state the efforts made to obtain the other party’s current address, and the form would also include a notice to the declarant that failure to diligently try to locate the other party’s current address may result in the court not hearing the motion.\n\n*Declaration Re Address Verification—Postjudgment Request to Modify a Child Custody, Visitation, or Child Support Order* (form FL-334) would serve as an optional address verification declaration that is required under Family Code section 215. Modeled after the existing form *Declaration in Support of Attorney’s Motion to Be Relieved as Counsel—Civil* (form MC-052), form FL-334 would require that the declarant verify the current address of the party being served within the 30 days immediately preceding the signing of the declaration and specify how he or she verified that address. Also, as with form MC-052, the declarant would be able to provide a service address that is the last known address of the party being served. Additional space would be provided for the declarant to state the efforts made to obtain the other party’s current address, and the form would also include a notice to the declarant that failure to diligently try to locate the other party’s current address may result in the court not hearing the motion.\n\nPostjudgment motions filed by the local child support agency will have a different address verification procedure because of the automated verification process used by the local child support agency as discussed below. A proof of service for use by the local child support agency is discussed below.\n\n**Governmental Child Support Forms**\n\nCurrently there is no Judicial Council governmental proof of service form for motions filed by the local child support agency. The local child support agencies (LCSAs) provide services in over 1.5 million child support cases statewide. The LCSAs generate all pleadings from a federally certified statewide child support case management system. This case management\n\nThe proposals have not been approved by the Judicial Council and are not intended to represent the views of the council, its Rules and Projects Committee, or its Policy Coordination and Liaison Committee. These proposals are circulated for comment purposes only.\n\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRule 3.1362. Motion to be relieved as counsel | Judicial Branch of California (https://courts.ca.gov/cms/rules/index/three/rule3_1362)\nciteturn1693search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; The motion to be relieved as counsel must be accompanied by a declaration on the Declaration in Support of Attorney's Motion to Be Relieved as Counsel-Civil (form MC-052). ... The proposed order relieving counsel must be prepared on the Order Granting Attorney's Motion to Be Relieved as Counsel-Civil (form MC-053) and must be lodged with the court with the moving papers.\n\nCalifornia Rules of Court 2026\n\n# Rule 3.1362. Motion to be relieved as counsel\n\n(a) Notice\n\nA notice of motion and motion to be relieved as counsel under Code of Civil Procedure section 284(2) must be directed to the client and must be made on the Notice of Motion and Motion to Be Relieved as Counsel-Civil (form MC-051).\n\n(Subd (a) amended effective January 1, 2007; previously amended effective July 1, 2000.)\n\n(b) Memorandum\n\nNotwithstanding any other rule of court, no memorandum is required to be filed or served with a motion to be relieved as counsel.\n\n(Subd (b) amended effective January 1, 2007; adopted effective July 1, 2000.)\n\n(c) Declaration\n\nThe motion to be relieved as counsel must be accompanied by a declaration on the Declaration in Support of Attorney's Motion to Be Relieved as Counsel-Civil (form MC-052). The declaration must state in general terms and without compromising the confidentiality of the attorney-client relationship why a motion under Code of Civil Procedure section 284(2) is brought instead of filing a consent under Code of Civil Procedure section 284(1).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/find-forms?page=1&query=civil)\nciteturn1693search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   * MC-050 * Substitution of Attorney—Civil (Without Court Order) Effective: January 1, 2009 ...   * MC-052 * Declaration in Support of Attorney's Motion to Be Relieved as Counsel—Civil Effective: January 1, 2007\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/find-forms/all-by-category)\nciteturn1693search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago;   * MC-050 * Substitution of